.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-APPLY 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-apply \- make the installation match an application's euclid/ folder
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap apply
.RB ( \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
|
.BR \-\-platform | \-P
.RI [ NAME ])
.RB [ \-\-directory | \-d
.IR FOLDER ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-prune ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads an application's
.I euclid/
folder, merges its files into one declaration, stores it beside the
artifact in the application's own bucket as
.IR <application-id>.euclid.json ,
and asks EAP to apply it. EAP applies the stored declaration again on every
create, update and redeploy of the application, so the folder is the
statement of record every time it is deployed.
.PP
With
.B \-\-dry\-run
nothing is stored or changed: EAP is handed the folder through
.B plan-infrastructure
and answers what it would do. It is the same code a deploy runs, so what a
dry run shows is what a deploy does. An EAP too old to know that action
refuses it, rather than applying anything.
.SH THE FOLDER
Every
.I *.json
directly in the folder is read and merged; how they are split is the
author's business.
.PP
.RS
.nf
{ "version": 1,
  "applicationId": "parsing",
  "creates": { "queues": [ {"name": "parsing-in", "visibility": 300,
                            "maxRetries": 5, "deadLetterQueue": "parsing-in-dlq"},
                           {"name": "parsing-in-dlq"} ] },
  "uses":    { "buckets": [ {"name": "transfer-server",
                             "access": ["subscribe", "read"],
                             "owner": "ftp-server"} ] } }
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B creates
is what the application owns. Every field but
.I name
is a setting. Queues take
.IR visibility ,
.IR maxRetries ,
.IR maxMessageLength ,
.IR delay ,
.IR retention ,
.I priority
and
.IR deadLetterQueue ;
topics take
.I maxMessageLength
and
.IR retention ;
tables take
.I partitionKey
(required),
.IR partitionKeyType ,
.I sortKey
and
.IR sortKeyType ,
fixed for the table's life;
routes take
.I path
(required),
.IR methods ,
.I authentication
and
.IR timeout ,
and always send to the application declaring them; buckets take none yet. A setting is applied when the object is made and
again to an existing one; a setting left out is left alone. An unknown key,
or a value out of range, is refused naming the file.
.PP
.B uses
is what somebody else owns, with the
.I access
the application needs:
.IR read ,
.IR write ,
.IR delete ,
.IR move ,
.I rename
or
.I subscribe
on a bucket,
.IR consume ,
.I produce
or
.I control
on a queue,
.I produce
or
.I subscribe
on a topic,
.I read
on a secret,
.I read
or
.I write
on a table. Nothing is created; something used that does not exist stops
the apply.
.SH WHAT IT DOES
.TP
.B create
An owned object that is not there is made, with its settings, and tagged
.I euclid:application
with the application's id.
.TP
.B update
An owned object that is there has the settings its file names brought into
line.
.TP
.B adopt
An object that exists untagged is claimed. One tagged for another
application is refused and the apply stops.
.TP
.B remove
An object this application owns and no longer declares is removed \- only
when empty.
.TP
.B keep
One that still holds messages, deliveries, objects or subscriptions is kept,
listed with what it holds, and stays owned.
.B \-\-no\-prune
keeps everything no longer declared.
.TP
.BR grant ", " revoke
The application's
.I access-*
grants are made exactly what the folder asks for: the levels named under
.BR uses ,
and everything on what it
.BR creates . Grants under any other role are never touched.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
The application the folder belongs to. A folder that names a different
.I applicationId
is refused. Required.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-platform " [" \fINAME\fR "]"
Apply the folder as a platform: the shared objects of the namespace that no
application owns, such as the bucket an FTP server writes into. It may only
create; what it owns is tagged
.IR platform:<name> ,
and applications reach it with
.BR uses .
The name defaults to
.IR platform .
The declaration is kept on the platform's record in EAP; see
.BR euclid-cli-eap-get-platform (1).
Instead of
.BR \-\-application\-id .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directory " " \fIFOLDER\fR
The folder.
.I \-\-dir
is the same. Defaults to
.IR ./euclid .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Say what would be done, and do none of it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prune
Keep owned objects that are no longer declared, even when they are empty.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print EAP's answer as JSON rather than one line per step.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Applied, or on a dry run, could be applied.
.TP
.B 1
The folder could not be read, or EAP could not apply it \- the message says
why. Nothing is half-applied out of a folder that did not parse.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap apply -n parsing -d ./euclid --dry-run
euclid-cli eap apply -n parsing -d ./euclid
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-get-platform (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-create-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-redeploy-application (1)
